1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,706
Let's turn these air duster photos into a fitted
Gridfinity tray

2
00:00:03,706 --> 00:00:07,262
in Pocketry. We'll trace and edit the outlines,
review the reference

3
00:00:07,262 --> 00:00:10,818
layout, then export a thin fit test and the
final multi-color

4
00:00:10,818 --> 00:00:11,788
3MF.

5
00:00:12,417 --> 00:00:16,806
The reference pack includes the air duster,
stacked adapters, and angled

6
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:21,195
nozzle, plus an alternate photo with two
objects. These copies have

7
00:00:21,195 --> 00:00:24,387
their display orientation preserved and embedded
metadata removed.

8
00:00:25,017 --> 00:00:28,679
Start in Trace, choose the air duster photo, and
let Pocketry

9
00:00:28,679 --> 00:00:32,340
find the four calibration markers. Keep the tool
and every marker

10
00:00:32,340 --> 00:00:34,005
visible in your source image.

11
00:00:34,617 --> 00:00:38,563
Accept the detected calibration sheet and apply
perspective correction. The corrected

12
00:00:38,563 --> 00:00:42,508
image is upright and has a real millimetre
scale. Check the

13
00:00:42,508 --> 00:00:46,096
printed calibration bar if you are preparing
your own photographs.

14
00:00:46,717 --> 00:00:50,190
Choose Set Region and drag a rectangle around
the duster. Leave

15
00:00:50,190 --> 00:00:53,663
a little breathing room around the tool, but
keep the printed

16
00:00:53,663 --> 00:00:56,188
markers and unrelated objects outside the
detection area.

17
00:00:56,817 --> 00:01:01,195
Open Outline and inspect the detected shape.
Outside silhouette is the

18
00:01:01,195 --> 00:01:05,573
default. Leave interior holes off here:
highlights, logos, and dark details

19
00:01:05,573 --> 00:01:08,757
inside the tool should not create extra cutouts.

20
00:01:09,383 --> 00:01:13,489
Adjust Sensitivity while watching the outline.
Before any manual edits, releasing

21
00:01:13,489 --> 00:01:17,594
the slider automatically detects it again. Look
for the physical edge

22
00:01:17,594 --> 00:01:21,327
of the tool, especially where a shadow touches
the body.

23
00:01:21,950 --> 00:01:25,634
Use Detail to remove unnecessary pixel-level
points, then choose Edit points

24
00:01:25,634 --> 00:01:29,318
and select the shape. Keep enough vertices to
preserve the corners

25
00:01:29,318 --> 00:01:31,997
and curves that matter for the tool's fit.

26
00:01:32,617 --> 00:01:36,379
Drag a misplaced vertex onto the actual edge.
Here the handle

27
00:01:36,379 --> 00:01:40,142
boundary needs a small correction. Work around
the silhouette at a

28
00:01:40,142 --> 00:01:43,221
useful zoom, rather than following every change
in brightness.

29
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:47,038
Click an outline edge to add a vertex where
another bend

30
00:01:47,038 --> 00:01:50,226
is needed. At the neck of the duster, an extra
control

31
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:52,834
point lets the outline follow the transition
more closely.

32
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:57,384
Right-click an unnecessary vertex to remove it.
Fewer well-placed points are

33
00:01:57,384 --> 00:02:01,317
often easier to refine. If a change makes the
outline worse,

34
00:02:01,317 --> 00:02:03,105
use Undo and try again.

35
00:02:03,717 --> 00:02:06,794
Choose Add to bin and give the trace a
meaningful name,

36
00:02:06,794 --> 00:02:09,871
such as Air Duster. Use Add and trace another
photo to

37
00:02:09,871 --> 00:02:12,109
queue this shape and continue with the adapters.

38
00:02:12,717 --> 00:02:16,448
Load the adapter-stack photograph and repeat the
calibration and perspective steps.

39
00:02:16,448 --> 00:02:20,180
A separate photo gives this narrow shape enough
detail to inspect

40
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:22,555
before adding it to the same bin.

41
00:02:23,183 --> 00:02:27,089
Set a region around the stack and examine the
first detection.

42
00:02:27,089 --> 00:02:30,995
This photo needs more attention: reflections and
shadows create an incorrect

43
00:02:30,995 --> 00:02:34,901
edge near the lower adapters. Automatic
detection is the starting point.

44
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:39,200
Adjust Sensitivity until the main silhouette is
close. For this pass,

45
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:42,883
I temporarily remove the margin so the edge is
easier to

46
00:02:42,883 --> 00:02:46,566
inspect, then use the point editor to correct
the remaining notch.

47
00:02:47,183 --> 00:02:50,694
Zoom into the lower adapter. The contour dives
into a bright

48
00:02:50,694 --> 00:02:54,205
area even though the solid tool continues across
it. Those inward

49
00:02:54,205 --> 00:02:57,396
points describe a reflection, not the outside of
the part.

50
00:02:58,017 --> 00:03:01,251
Remove the two inward vertices so the contour
follows the outside

51
00:03:01,251 --> 00:03:04,484
edge. Check the neighbouring segments after each
removal, and move or

52
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:07,718
add a point if the new line misses a real
corner.

53
00:03:08,350 --> 00:03:11,979
Restore the half-millimetre margin after
editing. Margin offsets the edited contour

54
00:03:11,979 --> 00:03:15,609
without detecting it again. This is a starting
allowance; the printed

55
00:03:15,609 --> 00:03:19,238
fit test will tell you whether your tools need
more or

56
00:03:19,238 --> 00:03:19,898
less room.

57
00:03:20,517 --> 00:03:24,086
Name the second shape Adapters and queue it for
the bin.

58
00:03:24,086 --> 00:03:27,656
Descriptive names make it much easier to select
the right pocket

59
00:03:27,656 --> 00:03:30,252
later, especially when a layout contains similar
accessories.

60
00:03:30,883 --> 00:03:34,487
Now load the angled nozzle photo, accept the
sheet calibration, and

61
00:03:34,487 --> 00:03:38,091
select the region around the nozzle. This is the
accessory for

62
00:03:38,091 --> 00:03:40,385
the upper-left pocket in the reference layout.

63
00:03:41,017 --> 00:03:45,015
The nozzle also has a reflective streak that the
detector follows

64
00:03:45,015 --> 00:03:49,014
inward. Select its contour, zoom in, and inspect
those vertices. The

65
00:03:49,014 --> 00:03:51,559
outside edge should enclose the solid nozzle.

66
00:03:52,183 --> 00:03:56,058
Right-click the vertices that run down the
reflection. This closes the

67
00:03:56,058 --> 00:03:59,933
false notch. Continue checking the tip, the
sloping body, and the

68
00:03:59,933 --> 00:04:03,808
mounting flange, moving points where the
physical edge needs a closer

69
00:04:03,808 --> 00:04:04,160
match.

70
00:04:04,783 --> 00:04:09,406
After moving, adding, or removing vertices,
changing Sensitivity asks before replacing

71
00:04:09,406 --> 00:04:14,029
your work. Choose Keep my edits to preserve the
contour. Replace

72
00:04:14,029 --> 00:04:18,652
manual edits runs detection again, and Undo can
recover the previous

73
00:04:18,652 --> 00:04:19,072
outline.

74
00:04:19,683 --> 00:04:23,594
Name this shape Angled Nozzle. Add the queued
shapes to the

75
00:04:23,594 --> 00:04:27,504
bin. You now have the photo-tracing workflow for
all three photographed

76
00:04:27,504 --> 00:04:30,348
tools, including the manual corrections needed
around reflections.

77
00:04:30,950 --> 00:04:34,665
Pocketry places the queued tools in the bin
designer. The first

78
00:04:34,665 --> 00:04:38,381
arrangement may use a larger bin. Check the size
and layout

79
00:04:38,381 --> 00:04:41,759
before assuming the automatically chosen
footprint matches your intended tray.

80
00:04:42,383 --> 00:04:46,488
Save this photo-tracing demonstration to the
project library before moving on.

81
00:04:46,488 --> 00:04:50,593
Download an editable project backup as well, so
the tracing work

82
00:04:50,593 --> 00:04:53,578
can be reopened independently of the reference
layout.

83
00:04:54,183 --> 00:04:59,097
For the exact reference design, open the
supplied editable Pocketry project.

84
00:04:59,097 --> 00:05:04,011
This checkpoint restores its saved outlines,
positions, and depths. The following

85
00:05:04,011 --> 00:05:08,924
layout uses that reference geometry, rather than
claiming fresh photo traces

86
00:05:08,924 --> 00:05:10,265
are numerically identical.

87
00:05:10,883 --> 00:05:15,005
In Size, use full pitch, four cells wide, four
cells long,

88
00:05:15,005 --> 00:05:19,128
and six point five units high. The displayed
outer size is

89
00:05:19,128 --> 00:05:23,250
one hundred sixty-seven point five millimetres
square, and about forty-nine point

90
00:05:23,250 --> 00:05:23,999
one high.

91
00:05:24,617 --> 00:05:28,421
Switch to Layout and open Arrange. The duster is
on the

92
00:05:28,421 --> 00:05:32,226
right, adapters in the middle, angled nozzle at
upper left, and

93
00:05:32,226 --> 00:05:36,030
USB cable along the left edge. The tutorial
corrects the reference

94
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:37,414
project's misleading accessory names.

95
00:05:38,017 --> 00:05:41,856
Select Air Duster. Its position is measured from
the bin centre:

96
00:05:41,856 --> 00:05:45,696
positive X moves right, and positive Y moves up.
Width and

97
00:05:45,696 --> 00:05:49,535
length describe the pocket before rotation. The
exact reference values are

98
00:05:49,535 --> 00:05:50,233
shown here.

99
00:05:50,850 --> 00:05:54,192
Enter a different X value to see the pocket move
across

100
00:05:54,192 --> 00:05:57,534
the layout. You can also drag it directly. Use
the numeric

101
00:05:57,534 --> 00:06:00,876
fields when you want a repeatable position
instead of estimating by

102
00:06:00,876 --> 00:06:01,180
eye.

103
00:06:01,783 --> 00:06:05,337
Return the duster to its reference position.
Check the space around

104
00:06:05,337 --> 00:06:08,890
neighbouring pockets and the outside wall. Use
width and length or

105
00:06:08,890 --> 00:06:12,443
the layout handles to adjust the fit, keeping
the aspect ratio

106
00:06:12,443 --> 00:06:13,412
locked when appropriate.

107
00:06:14,017 --> 00:06:18,068
For the duster, use a fixed depth of thirty-nine
millimetres. Pocketry

108
00:06:18,068 --> 00:06:22,120
shows the remaining floor beside the depth
diagram. The reference also

109
00:06:22,120 --> 00:06:26,172
uses one-millimetre outline and top rounding,
with a one point two

110
00:06:26,172 --> 00:06:27,277
millimetre bottom fillet.

111
00:06:27,883 --> 00:06:32,016
Select Adapters and check its position and size.
This pocket uses

112
00:06:32,016 --> 00:06:36,149
a thirty-four millimetre depth and a small
rotation. Use the supplied

113
00:06:36,149 --> 00:06:40,281
project for the full-precision values; the
controls show rounded numbers for

114
00:06:40,281 --> 00:06:40,657
readability.

115
00:06:41,283 --> 00:06:45,631
Select Angled Nozzle. It belongs above the cable
pocket, rotated to

116
00:06:45,631 --> 00:06:49,978
fit the available space. The reference uses a
thirty-four millimetre depth.

117
00:06:49,978 --> 00:06:53,535
Keep this nozzle-shaped outline when reproducing
the supplied design.

118
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,674
The long, narrow pocket on the left is for the
USB

119
00:06:57,674 --> 00:07:01,198
cable. Here we keep its original outline for an
exact layout

120
00:07:01,198 --> 00:07:04,722
match. An oblong finger pocket could provide an
approximate alternative in

121
00:07:04,722 --> 00:07:05,683
a new design.

122
00:07:06,283 --> 00:07:10,088
For the cable pocket, the depth mode is Keep
floor thickness,

123
00:07:10,088 --> 00:07:13,893
set to seven millimetres. This controls the
material left underneath the

124
00:07:13,893 --> 00:07:17,697
pocket, rather than asking you to calculate a
cut depth yourself.

125
00:07:18,317 --> 00:07:21,759
Open Finger access. Add hole creates an opening
you can position

126
00:07:21,759 --> 00:07:25,201
independently of a tool pocket. The reference
uses an oblong deep

127
00:07:25,201 --> 00:07:28,644
scoop across the bin and a separate round deep
scoop beside

128
00:07:28,644 --> 00:07:29,270
the nozzle.

129
00:07:29,883 --> 00:07:34,098
For the cross-bin opening, choose Oblong deep
scoop. Set its diameter,

130
00:07:34,098 --> 00:07:38,313
length, total depth, and rotation, then position
it across the tools.

131
00:07:38,313 --> 00:07:42,528
The broad rounded opening gives your fingers
room to lift the

132
00:07:42,528 --> 00:07:42,911
contents.

133
00:07:43,517 --> 00:07:47,119
The second opening uses Deep scoop, with a
twenty-two millimetre diameter

134
00:07:47,119 --> 00:07:50,721
and thirty-three point five millimetre depth.
Position it at the top

135
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:54,324
of the nozzle pocket so the accessory can be
gripped and

136
00:07:54,324 --> 00:07:54,651
lifted.

137
00:07:55,283 --> 00:07:58,858
In Materials, use a gray body with black pocket
floors and

138
00:07:58,858 --> 00:08:02,433
a black rim top. The reference uses a zero point
six

139
00:08:02,433 --> 00:08:06,008
millimetre floor-color region and a one point
two five millimetre rim-color

140
00:08:06,008 --> 00:08:06,333
region.

141
00:08:06,950 --> 00:08:10,599
Switch to the 3D view and inspect the complete
bin.

142
00:08:10,599 --> 00:08:14,248
Look for unwanted intersections, thin walls, and
access to each tool.

143
00:08:14,248 --> 00:08:17,898
The preview helps review geometry, but the
physical fit test is

144
00:08:17,898 --> 00:08:19,225
still the next step.

145
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:23,082
Open Check fit and choose the complete surface
fit test. Set

146
00:08:23,082 --> 00:08:26,314
the thickness to one point two millimetres, then
save the S

147
00:08:26,314 --> 00:08:29,546
T L. This exports the whole pocket layout as one
thin

148
00:08:29,546 --> 00:08:29,839
plate.

149
00:08:30,450 --> 00:08:33,578
Print that plate and try the actual tools in it
before

150
00:08:33,578 --> 00:08:36,707
printing the full bin. It checks the openings
and finger-hole layout

151
00:08:36,707 --> 00:08:39,835
together. It does not test pocket depth or how
the base

152
00:08:39,835 --> 00:08:40,688
fits a baseplate.

153
00:08:41,317 --> 00:08:45,336
Once the fit is satisfactory, open Export and
choose Save three

154
00:08:45,336 --> 00:08:49,355
M F. This exports the complete printable bin at
print quality,

155
00:08:49,355 --> 00:08:52,279
including its base, walls, pockets, and stacking
lip.

156
00:08:52,883 --> 00:08:56,958
Choose Multi-color 3MF to preserve the separate
body, floor,

157
00:08:56,958 --> 00:09:01,034
and rim regions. Open the result in your slicer
and verify

158
00:09:01,034 --> 00:09:05,109
the objects and filament assignments before
slicing; color handling can vary

159
00:09:05,109 --> 00:09:05,850
between slicers.

160
00:09:06,450 --> 00:09:10,455
Keep the matching dot Pocketry dot JSON file

161
00:09:10,455 --> 00:09:14,459
alongside the printable model. Each model export
also downloads this editable

162
00:09:14,459 --> 00:09:18,464
backup. If the browser asks, allow multiple
downloads and confirm that

163
00:09:18,464 --> 00:09:19,556
both files arrived.

164
00:09:20,183 --> 00:09:23,858
The files are ready: clean reference photos, the
corrected editable layout,

165
00:09:23,858 --> 00:09:27,532
a thin surface fit test, and the final multi-
color three M

166
00:09:27,532 --> 00:09:31,206
F. Print the fit test first, refine the
allowance if needed,

167
00:09:31,206 --> 00:09:33,210
and then make the complete tray.
